# FLUSH statements (server-side cache/state maintenance).
#
# `FLUSH <target>` tells a MySQL server to flush internal caches or state
# (TABLES, PRIVILEGES, STATUS, LOGS, the table cache held under a global read
# lock, ...). It appears in mysqldump output and DB-admin / import flows. This
# single-node engine keeps none of that server-side state, so the front-end
# accepts `FLUSH <target>` as a no-op that reports success, matching MySQL's
# empty OK. Bare `FLUSH` (no target) stays a syntax error, as in MySQL.
#
# Only universally-accepted targets are tested: MySQL 8.4 removed `FLUSH HOSTS`,
# so that variant is left out. `FLUSH TABLES WITH READ LOCK` takes a global read
# lock on a real server, so it is released with `UNLOCK TABLES` to avoid blocking
# later statements.
#
# Expected to pass against both turso-mysql-server and a real mysqld (8.4).

statement ok
FLUSH TABLES

statement ok
FLUSH PRIVILEGES

statement ok
FLUSH STATUS

statement ok
FLUSH LOGS

# Case-insensitive, like every other keyword.
statement ok
flush tables

statement ok
FLUSH TABLES WITH READ LOCK

statement ok
UNLOCK TABLES

# A normal query still works after the flush/unlock round-trip.
query
SELECT 1
----
1

# Bare `FLUSH` with no target is rejected on both engines.
statement error
FLUSH
